FILE codex-cold.md
     1	**The new system is not ready to replace the running pipeline. Gate 1 still fails with Astra, and Gate 2 has not passed.** The clearest Gate 1 problem is that Astra sometimes refuses to write a question even when Alex has already said the topic is one question: for example, total semiconductor sales and the AI-chip portion of that total.
     2	
     3	I recommend one next step: **a $0 audit and design of a general explanation of Alex’s existing “two sides of one bet” decision**, using stored examples outside the 44. It must cover both a total and its part, and a figure with a condition. It must also check cases that really are two separate questions. Only if that audit produces a defensible candidate and a sufficiently informative test should the council decide whether to spend on a small comparison.
     4	
     5	This is an independent read-only review of the records. I did **not** run a new council, make a model call, or edit the record. The “council” findings below distinguish my conclusions from the earlier recorded discussions.
     6	
     7	### 1. Where each gate stands
     8	
     9	| Gate | Measured status | What that means |
    10	|---|---|---|
    11	| **1. Answer choices** | **Failed.** Astra’s 132 sends passed lines 1, 3 and 6, but had **7 unjustified refusals** on line 4. Lines 2 and 5 differ under the two treatments of unresolved reader disputes. | The failure does not depend on resolving the deadline disputes: line 4 fails either way. [Astra report](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-astra-2026-11-17/run-1/report-output.txt:1), [D649](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8938) |
    12	| **2. Judge on the new questions** | **Not passed.** All three draws completed and all 26 eligible questions were judged. Figure and yes/no unsupported-answer limits were met. Only **4 comparable controls** had the necessary material, against **8 required**; under the strictest reading, **3 of 24** final answers were unsupported, above the one-in-ten limit. | The control test is **unexercised**, not a demonstrated failure of all other questions. The 236 daily judgements repeat questions and are not 236 independent tests. This was measured on the earlier Luna answer choices, not an accepted Astra configuration. [Stage B result](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/stage_b/STAGE_B_RESULT.md:1) |
    13	| **3. End-to-end comparison** | **Owed.** The required comparison and a numerical definition of “beats” remain open. Earlier comparisons are diagnostics, not acceptance. | No evidence yet establishes that the complete new system beats the running one. [PRD](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/PRD.md:126) |
    14	| **4. Use on unseen news** | **Not an accepted gate yet.** One fresh-week diagnostic covered **2 of 5** major matters and had **6 of 7** last answers supported, versus **10 of 15** for the other system; its proposed limits are not Alex’s accepted limits. | This small diagnostic is a warning, not a final comparison or an accepted gate result. [PRD](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/PRD.md:394) |
    15	| **5. Integration and release** | **Incomplete.** Some integration has been built and tested, but the full production path and release acceptance remain owed. | It cannot make up for the preceding gates. I have not reviewed or proposed further work on pages or live service. [PRD](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/PRD.md:126) |
    16	
    17	For Gate 2 specifically, the figure unsupported rates were **3/88, 3/95 and 10/95**, each below its respective baseline-plus-five-point limit; yes/no rates were **1/117, 1/122 and 6/122**, all within 5%. The strict final-answer result was **3/24**, with two “insufficient” answers reported separately. [Stage B result](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/stage_b/STAGE_B_RESULT.md:12)
    18	
    19	### 2. Astra Gate 1: accounting and disputed cases
    20	
    21	**The seven refusals are real misses under the recorded reading.** They comprise three refusals of HBM shipment growth with a shortage condition, three of total semiconductor revenue with its AI-chip portion, and one of combined HBM capacity with a timing condition. The report gives **26 justified and 7 unjustified refusals out of 33**. [Astra report](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-astra-2026-11-17/run-1/report-output.txt:20), [D649](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8955)
    22	
    23	Alex already settled the first two shapes. He answered **“算一个题，第三处不改”**—count it as one question—and the resulting decision explicitly names “the shortage beside the shipments” and “the AI-chip share beside the chip total” as second sides of one bet, kept as context and shown as not judged. That supports asking the main question in these named cases; it does **not** establish a universal rule that every total always takes priority over every part. The capacity case also needs its own topic-specific reading rather than a blanket rule. [D436](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4847)
    24	
    25	The two accounting treatments are:
    26	
    27	- **Unresolved points counted in Astra’s favour:** line 2 passes; line 5 passes with **40 of 44 right**, including **31 with written choices**.
    28	- **Unresolved points counted against Astra:** line 2 misses by **one**; line 5 has **35 of 44 right**, but only **26 with written choices**, below the required 28.
    29	- Line 6 passes in both treatments: the favourable direction agrees for all **9** applicable topics, and the due day agrees for **32 of 33** applicable topics. Consistency does not establish that a due day is correct. [Astra report](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-astra-2026-11-17/run-1/report-output.txt:1)
    30	
    31	The disputed points are **not all one unresolved owner decision**:
    32	
    33	1. **Annual-figure deadlines: the instrument is internally ambiguous.** The reader form says a year’s figure with no stated publication day takes the code-set date, “**60 days after the company’s fiscal year-end**”; its detailed deadline mark also says an existing obligation “**is due at its next annual filing**.” [Reader brief](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md:68), [deadline mark](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md:98)
    34	
    35	   D414 says for a **one-year figure** whose date would otherwise be estimated, code sets fiscal year-end plus 60 days; “**A day the topic or the item states is kept**.” Alex accepted code setting it. Where the disputed answer really is a year-end figure and no exact publication day is stated, D414 supports the code-set day, not an approximate February date substituted by a reader. [D414](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4568)
    36	
    37	   The third readers demonstrate the collision: one failed an April 1 date because the filing was around February; the other held it because no exact filing day was stated and April 1 was the code-set date. [Codex ruling](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-astra-2026-11-17/run-1/third-reader/rulings-codex-1.jsonl:1), [Claude ruling](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-astra-2026-11-17/run-1/third-reader/rulings-claude-1.jsonl:1)
    38	
    39	2. **A stated event deadline is separately governed by D403.** Alex chose “**保持更严**”: use the date in the topic or article, or the last day of the topic’s period; the model chooses no other day. That settles the rule for an event, but applying it still requires deciding what that particular topic asks and which period it names. [D403](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4414)
    40	
    41	3. **The other disagreements must not be silently converted into deadline rulings.** For example, the two third readers split over whether an omitted Nvidia spending share was already adequately described in the unjudged-parts list. They also split on whether a 2027 or 2028 shipping deadline followed the topic. Those are different questions from the annual-filing conflict. [Codex rulings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-astra-2026-11-17/run-1/third-reader/rulings-codex-3.jsonl:1), [Claude rulings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-astra-2026-11-17/run-1/third-reader/rulings-claude-3.jsonl:1)
    42	
    43	The fourth Luna reading had line 1 missed by **1**, line 3 by **2**, and line 4 by **3** on the favourable treatment; its stricter treatment had **5, 4 and 3** respectively. This makes Astra’s recorded improvement on lines 1 and 3 useful evidence, but Astra is worse on the seven recorded refusals, and this is not a controlled production comparison. [Luna report](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/stage_a/result-4/report.txt:1), [Astra report](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-astra-2026-11-17/run-1/report-output.txt:1)
    44	
    45	### 3. What the latest diagnostic shows
    46	
    47	The D651 test compared the unchanged Astra request with the same request plus two sentences derived from D436. There were **11 topics, two sends per version**, for **44 sends and $1.49**. The two readers had classified **six topics as questions** and **five as justified refusals** before seeing the answers. [D651](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8991), [classifications](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/score-output.txt:1)
    48	
    49	Only **two** of the six target topics actually exercised the refusal problem in the unchanged version, below the pre-set minimum of **three**, so the registered result is **inconclusive**:
    50	
    51	- On **pool-7**, the unchanged version refused twice; the edited version wrote two questions that neither reader marked faulty.
    52	- On **pool-17**, both versions refused twice. The edited version did not recover the total-and-split question.
    53	- All five justified-refusal controls held in the edited version.
    54	- The strict topic-level accounting flagged pool-17 as worse on the agreed count: **two** edited failures versus **one** unchanged failure. One unchanged refusal was disputed, so this is a reader-count difference, not proof that the edit changed the written behaviour. [Score](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/score-output.txt:63), [per-topic results](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/score-output.txt:110), [round 44](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/challenge-44.md:5)
    55	
    56	This is **a promising observed recovery on one topic**, not proof that the sentences fix all “figure plus condition” cases. Likewise, the failure on one total-and-split topic is evidence against assuming that class is fixed, not proof that every such case will fail. The prediction that a new Gate 1 run would still miss by three or four is plausible, but it is a prediction, not a measured result. The pre-set rule correctly stops the gate rerun. [D652](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9020), [test rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/second_side_probe.py:227)
    57	
    58	The model comparisons also do not establish a cheap substitute. Sol improved agreed failures from **13 to 5 of 78**, but exceeded the refusal bound. The paired Astra screens recorded **4 of 78** agreed failures, with the complementary screen closing under its own rule. Opus and DeepSeek each had only one draw per model and were inconclusive; Luna itself moved from **7 to 1** agreed failures between those one-draw trials. [Sol result](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/model/RESULT.md:3), [Astra result](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/astra/RESULT-REST.md:10), [Opus](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/opus/RESULT.md:1), [DeepSeek](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/deepseek/RESULT.md:1)
    59	
    60	### 4. Production blockers, in order
    61	
    62	1. **Gate 1 is failed and the written questions also need substantive scrutiny.** The seven Astra refusals alone block the gate. Separately, a source-backed review found **11 material issues in 51 Astra-written questions**: four signed-deal completion dates, three events due before their settling report, three widened periods, and one invented measure. These are not 11 independent topics, and the later Rubin trace attributes its widened period to the topic’s own period field. They show why a zero hard-line count would not alone establish usable questions. [Astra review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/astra-review/RESULT.md:1), [D638](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8687)
    63	
    64	2. **Gate 2 lacks enough comparable controls and has a strict final-answer miss.** A repeat send of the same judge cannot manufacture four more comparable questions with the required material. Gate 1 must produce usable, comparable questions and allow the relevant evidence to reach the judge; even then, the final-answer quality needs to be measured. The judge’s internals are outside this review. [Stage B result](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/stage_b/STAGE_B_RESULT.md:79), [PRD](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/PRD.md:440)
    65	
    66	3. **The later gates remain owed**, including an agreed comparison standard for Gate 3 and an accepted Gate 4. They are not the next work in this review’s scope. [PRD](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/PRD.md:126)
    67	
    68	4. **Cost at the requested scale is unknown.** The stored November 13 clean run charged **$0.028, $0.029 and $0.047** for the three Luna answer-choice components; Astra’s screens averaged roughly **$0.037–$0.050 per send**, and the Astra Gate 1 run cost **$4.74**. Alex’s concern about 1,000 asset categories cannot be answered from one NVDA period: the number of new questions, article volume, overlap and retries at that scale are unknown. A simple cost per question is useful for budgeting, not a production forecast. [Spend file](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/spend-by-component.json:29), [Opus cost comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/opus/RESULT.md:52), [D649](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8938)
    69	
    70	**Authority:** Alex chose Astra as the candidate and authorized the recorded Gate 1 launch; that choice does not waive the gates. Under D439, when he is inactive the council may reach and record reversible decisions, including opening a new version of this frozen step. It cannot change the frozen ruler, exceed the applicable spending authority, or deploy, publish, push or commit without being asked. A new frozen-ruler version remains Alex’s decision. The recorded $40 ceiling was Claude’s limit for that period, not a figure Alex personally set; later explicitly authorized spending must be accounted for separately. [D648](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8914), [D439](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4871), [D544](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:6616)
    71	
    72	Alex may of course settle genuinely open product questions himself. The council should not describe every question as “only Alex can decide” when D439 delegates reversible choices, nor treat its own interpretation as an Alex decision.
    73	
    74	### 5. One next step, then the step after it
    75	
    76	**Next: a $0, read-only source and request audit of the D436 candidate.** Compare the full request, schema and closing check with D436 and the stored topics: the two recovered/refused diagnostic topics, true separate-question controls, and the named total-and-part and shortage cases. Produce a general, cross-domain candidate only if the existing decision supports its boundary. In the same audit, resolve the reader-form conflict against D403 and D414 for each applicable case, without changing the frozen gold or retroactively declaring a pass.
    77	
    78	- **Falsifier:** the proposed wording would make a genuinely separate question disappear, change a justified refusal into an unsound question, conflict with the existing request or decisions, or has no outside cases capable of testing the intended failure. Any of these stops that candidate.
    79	- **Cost:** **$0 in model calls**; the cost is reviewer time. No model send is part of this step.
    80	- **What changes the plan:** if a source review shows the alleged recovery chose an unsupported measure, if the broader rule would merge separate bets, or if there are too few suitable outside cases, do not spend on that candidate. Return the unresolved boundary to the council with the examples and choose the cheaper reversible course. The earlier cold reviewer specifically warned that pool-7’s DDR4 choice needed source checking before calling it a confirmed recovery. [Round 43 review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/codex-challenge-43.md:1)
    81	
    82	**After that:** if the audit survives, have the council pre-register and price a **small matched comparison outside the 44**, with enough independently classified target cases, justified-refusal controls, both reader countings, and source review of every recovered question. A positive small test would justify considering a Gate 1 rerun; it would not itself pass the gate. The 44 have already informed the proposed repair, so any rerun on them must be labelled an **exposed acceptance set**, followed by fresh-material evidence before generalizing. [Round 40 review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/codex-challenge-40.md:3)
    83	
    84	### 6. Errors and overclaims in the record
    85	
    86	- **“The two sentences fix” a class is too strong.** D652’s heading and round 44 say the edit fixes a figure-plus-condition shape. The registered result is inconclusive, with only one recovered topic and two sends. The narrower statement is that it recovered pool-7 in this diagnostic. [D652](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9020), [score](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/score-output.txt:242)
    87	
    88	- **The diagnostic’s population description is inaccurate.** D651 says the 11 topics came from the 48 outside both the 44 and the 26, but its own topic file identifies **three as `26-set`**. They remain outside the 44, but they are not all outside the earlier 26. [D651](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8999), [topic list](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/topics-11.json:42)
    89	
    90	- **The decision page overstates the reader-control conclusion.** “The readers are not too harsh” goes beyond the reference test: the control found no confirmed reader error under its rule, but the careful writer had **four of 26** faults when unresolved points were counted against it. It does not prove the readers’ thresholds are generally correct. [Decision page](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16.md:125), [D624](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8405)
    91	
    92	- **The request contains a real deadline conflict.** It requires the due day to be “on or after” publication of settling material, but also sets an event at the end of its period. Those can be different days. Its blanket next-filing instruction for obligations also reaches completion events, while D403 governs event dates. This matters downstream: a consistent date can still close a question before the evidence arrives. [Request](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:19), [source review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/astra-review/RESULT.md:10)
    93	
    94	- **The PRD is stale on the selected model.** It still says the step uses Luna, while D648 records Alex’s later choice of Astra for the candidate. The decision page also still describes the Astra gate launch as unsent. These are historical documents, but a reader using them as current status would be misled. [PRD](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/PRD.md:338), [decision page](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16.md:166), [D648](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8914)
    95	
    96	- **“Reasoning effort is not the lever” is broader than the experiment.** On this request and 26 topics, medium and high produced **13 and 14** agreed failed sends out of 78; the registered comparison closes. It does not establish that reasoning effort cannot help another request or population. [Effort result](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/effort/RESULT.md:10)
    97	
    98	The immediate decision is therefore to diagnose and audit the known refusal mechanism at no API cost, while preserving the failed result and the unresolved measurement disputes. The record does not yet support claiming that the edited request, Astra, or the complete system is production-ready.
