    "outcome": "The split of big-four hyperscaler 2027 AI accelerator spending (and of the 19.5 GW of incremental compute capacity projected for 2027) between custom ASIC racks and Nvidia GPU racks, measured against the item's cost-per-gigawatt estimates of $6\u201311 billion for Broadcom/Marvell ASIC racks versus roughly $19 billion for Nvidia GB300-based racks and $25 billion under Vera Rubin.",
    "period": "Calendar 2027 \u2014 set by the item: Morgan Stanley forecasts the big four will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure 'next year' and add 19.5 GW of capacity, the period in which the ASIC-versus-GPU mix plays out.",
    "opens_as": "shared_quantity",
    "settled_by": "Broadcom's and Marvell's disclosed custom AI accelerator (XPU) revenue in their quarterly results and annual reports covering calendar 2027, alongside the hyperscalers' own capex disclosures for 2027."
  },
  "ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it": {
    "object": "custom-silicon",
    "kind": "theme",
    "focus": "The split of big-four hyperscaler 2027 AI accelerator spending (and of the 19.5 GW of incremental compute capacity projected for 2027) between custom ASIC racks and Nvidia GPU racks, measured against the item's cost-per-gigawatt estimates of $6\u201311 billion for Broadcom/Marvell ASIC racks versus roughly $19 billion for Nvidia GB300-based racks and $25 billion under Vera Rubin.",
    "question": "What percentage of Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Microsoft's combined calendar-2027 AI accelerator spending will go to custom ASIC accelerators designed by Broadcom and Marvell, as established by those designers' quarterly results and annual reports covering calendar 2027 and the hyperscalers' 2027 spending disclosures?",
    "year": "2027",
    "due_day": "2028-03-30",
    "rungs": [
      {
        "label": "below 10 percent",
        "condition": "below 10 percent"
      },
      {
        "label": "from 10 percent to below 20 percent",
        "condition": "from 10 percent to below 20 percent"
      },
      {
        "label": "from 20 percent to below 35 percent",
        "condition": "from 20 percent to below 35 percent (no reference figure when the question opened)"
      },
      {
        "label": "from 35 percent to below 50 percent",
        "condition": "from 35 percent to below 50 percent"
      },
      {
        "label": "50 percent or more",
        "condition": "50 percent or more"
      }
    ]
  },
  "other_fields": {
    "not_judged": [
      {
        "part": "The split of the projected 19.5 GW of incremental compute capacity between custom ASIC racks and Nvidia GPU racks.",
        "why": "Capacity share is a separate physical measure, whereas the main tracked figure is spending share."
      },
      {
        "part": "Whether custom ASICs take a growing share.",
        "why": "Growth requires a comparison with an earlier period, whereas the judged figure is the share for calendar 2027 alone."
      },
      {
        "part": "Whether custom ASIC gains come at Nvidia's expense.",
        "why": "Nvidia's corresponding share loss or displaced sales is a separate competitive outcome, not the custom ASIC spending share itself."
      },
      {
        "part": "Lower cost per gigawatt, including $6\u201311 billion for Broadcom/Marvell ASIC racks versus roughly $19 billion for Nvidia GB300 racks and $25 billion under Vera Rubin.",
        "why": "These cost comparisons motivate the topic but measure infrastructure economics rather than the tracked spending share."
      },
      {
        "part": "The forecast that the big four will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure in 2027.",
        "why": "Total AI infrastructure spending is a separate, broader figure than the allocation of AI accelerator spending."
      }
    ],
    "reference": null,
    "favourable_end": "n/a",
    "favourable_why": "",
    "ruled_out_by": null,
    "due_day_basis": "expected_publication"
  },
  "to_rule": [
    {
      "id": "122-L",
      "mark": "L",
      "reader_a": "holds",
      "reader_b": "fails",
      "reader_b_reason": "The question leaves out NVIDIA GPU racks' share of accelerator spending without listing that share in not_judged."
    }
  ]
}

codex
**DISAGREE on the draft and the proposed test as written; agree on the $0 audit as the next step.** The smallest correction is to treat the refusal mechanism as a strong hypothesis, narrow the draft so it does not automatically choose every total over its part, and test the candidate against the unchanged request with a zero-fault rule.

For example, Alex settled that chip-industry sales and their AI-chip portion are one question. That does not necessarily mean a question whose central subject is a particular product’s share should always be changed into a question about the whole company’s sales.

1. **Due days: agree, with the recount labelled as a counterfactual.** The 12 disputed obligation-amount cases are annual-report figures with no exact publication day stated. D414 explicitly makes the code-set date apply to a year’s figure whose date would otherwise be estimated, and Alex was told it could close about a month after the expected filing. The fiscal-year note’s “around February” is an estimate, even though it also tells the model to use that date. That instruction adds to the conflict but does not override D414. [D414](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4568), [example packet](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-astra-2026-11-17/run-1/third-reader/ruling-packet-1.json:5)

   The scratch recount reports **40/31** under the favourable treatment and **39/30** under the strict treatment, so line 5 passes on both after applying D414 to those 12 points. Line 2 remains disputed on the Nvidia-share listing. The saved reading should remain as recorded; this is an application of the decision beside it, not a replacement reading or a Gate 1 pass. [Recount](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/d414-counterfactual-report.txt:1)

   On that remaining listing point, I would retain the dispute: “at Nvidia’s expense” and “corresponding share loss” describe competitive loss, but the topic explicitly names Nvidia GPU racks’ share. Whether that is adequately listed is not settled by D414. [Packet](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-astra-2026-11-17/run-1/third-reader/ruling-packet-3.json)

2. **Mechanism: plausible and well supported, not yet proved.** The inserted sentence retains “decline only when no part is the main one,” and the model gave essentially that reason for the total-and-part refusals. The request therefore permits the observed refusal despite the instruction to keep a second side as context. But the text and answer do not establish which instruction caused the model’s choice; the model has not given its account, and pool-7 moved while pool-17 did not. [Inserted text](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/second_side_probe.py:34), [diagnostic](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/score-output.txt:110)

   D436 supports implementing the **named same-bet relationship**, including the three examples put to Alex. I would **not accept the draft as written**: “the total, the figure or the amount is the question” can impose a new priority based on grammar when the topic actually tracks the part or timing. “A forecast’s timing beside the amount” also needs its boundary checked against D436 rather than assumed equivalent. Keep the draft as audit material, not an adopted rule. [D436](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4847), [cases shown to Alex](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-diagnosis-2026-11-12/gate1/PREREAD-RESULT.md:8)

   The additional two `u3/clean-10` refusals are a **D436-based concern**, not two extra settled failures in the original count: both initial readers held them justified. Report seven recorded misses and the two potentially misread refusals separately until independently adjudicated. [Astra report](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-astra-2026-11-17/run-1/report-output.txt:46)

3. **Where to test: agree to use the exposed cases for a small verification, but not the proposed design unchanged.** Alex’s rulings make those cases valuable checks of whether the step follows his decision. They do not make them independent evidence of general performance, particularly after the failures shaped the edit.

   After the $0 audit, I would compare **fresh unchanged and edited sends** on the four named targets and the eight controls, with equal draws per version. Read every replacement against its source, including whether the chosen question and unjudged parts are sound. Count lost answers, any unjustified refusal, and **any** new hard-line fault—including a disputed fault in the strict treatment—as failures. Require controls to remain justified and no material harm. The proposed rule allowing one or two bad sends on a target would be useful descriptive information, but it is too weak as permission to proceed directly to a zero-fault gate.

   Pre-price the larger paired test, including retries and calls in flight; **28 sends and ~$1** describe the proposed edited-only test, not the paired comparison. If the paired test passes its prewritten rule, return to the council for the exposed 44-topic run, its budget and authority. Fresh-material evidence is still needed before claiming generality.

4. **Record corrections: agree as proposed, with two qualifications.**

   - D649 should identify **12** obligation-amount splits, D414’s application, the separate listing dispute, and the fact that not every initial reader failed each refusal. Preserve the original outcome and label the recount.
   - D651’s two sentences are an implementation **derived from** D436, not Alex’s verbatim words. Its population description also needs correction: three controls are marked as members of the earlier 26-topic set. [Topic list](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/topics-11.json:42)
   - D652 should say **“recovered pool-7”**, disclose the fired edited-worse flag and retain the inconclusive verdict. One unchanged pool-17 refusal was disputed rather than agreed unjustified. [Score](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-7/score-output.txt:77)
   - Mark the PRD and decision page as stale rather than treating their historical text as current. The frozen request remains unadopted; Astra is the chosen candidate, not a passed production configuration.

5. **Cost: your correction to the 2–3× claim is right; 6–7× is a rough estimate, not a measured full-system ratio.** I checked the archive: `c1` has **15 distinct topics in each of three passes**, with **16, 16 and 17 attempts** including retries. Thus 49 attempts are not 49 production questions. At the screen’s **$0.037 per Astra send**, roughly 16–17 attempts imply **$0.042–$0.045 per day** over 14 days. The reviewer’s claim that this component alone costs 2–3 times the whole running pipeline therefore does not follow. [Astra screen](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/astra/RESULT.md:3), [archive](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/c1/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:1)

   The completed clean article-reading charges are **$2.36–$2.51 per draw**, about **$0.17–$0.18/day**; running-pipeline draws are **$0.53–$0.66**, about **$0.038–$0.047/day**. Adding the estimated Astra step and the stored matching and judging charges gives approximately **$0.25–$0.27/day**, or roughly **5–7 times** the running draw’s daily cost using those observed ranges. The clean judge and other components must still be checked for repeated measurement work before calling that a production cost. The stopped `c2` article run is excluded from a normal completed-draw estimate. [Component charges](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/spend-by-component.json:13)

   Tell Alex this is a **one-company, 14-day historical estimate** with Astra substituted using a different trial’s average; it does not forecast 1,000 assets or measure overlap, caching and retries at that scale.

6. **For Alex:** the cost estimate belongs in the report. D592 says a **new reference for the existing Gate 2 control line is Alex’s**; the current four-control result cannot pass it. But do not state that a new reference is the *only logically possible route*: D592 also records an unproven two-step route to an eighth comparable control, outside this review’s scope. A new reference and any change to the frozen ruler are not actions the council can silently take. [D592](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:7715)

   No additional owner ruling is needed for the 12 D414 cases or D436’s named examples. The unresolved event-postponement boundary remains available for Alex to override; the council has a reversible interim position. Changes to the frozen ruler, deployment, and any spending beyond the applicable authorization remain outside the council’s unilateral authority. [D439](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4871)

**DISAGREE:** conduct the $0 whole-request and boundary audit first. If it yields a sourced candidate, use a paired, exposed verification with strict no-new-fault controls; decide separately on the gate rerun.
